A Coq formalization of a simplified version of the Ur programming language,
with a machine-checked proof of its core soundness properties.

Build and check:

  make          # coqc via coq_makefile
  make check    # coqchk (re-checks the proof TERMS in Coq's trusted kernel,
                # independently of the tactic engine) + Assumptions.v, which
                # pins the theorem STATEMENTS and diffs the axiom report
                # against the Assumptions.expected golden

CI runs `make check` on every push, so this cannot silently rot again. It was
previously pinned to Coq 8.3pl2 (2011) and had been unbuildable for about a
decade; reviving it needed one tactic rename, `elimtype False` -> `exfalso`.
Tested with Coq 8.19.2.

SCOPE -- what a reader may and may not conclude.

This is a formalization of "Featherweight Ur": a core calculus, not the
language the compiler implements, and not the compiler.

PROVED, mechanically, in Coq 8.19.2:

  A denotational model (cDen/eDen) of a PHOAS-encoded calculus with four kinds
  (Type, Name, k -> k, {k}), type-level records with concatenation, singletons
  and map, a declarative type-equality judgment (deq), a declarative row-
  disjointness judgment (disj), and guarded types.  Against that model:

    subs_correct      substitution is meaning-preserving
    deq_correct       deq is sound: derivable equality implies equal denotations
    disj_correct      disj is sound: a derivable [fs1 ~ fs2] implies the two
                      rows never map the same name to Some
    cut_disjoint      cutting a name out of a disjoint row preserves disjointness
    name_eq_dec_refl  decidable name equality is reflexive on the nose

  and, load-bearing, the interpreter

    eDen : forall t, exp dvar tDen t -> tDen t

  is DEFINABLE as a total Coq function.  That is the soundness statement: every
  well-typed Featherweight-Ur term denotes a value of its denoted type.  This is
  semantic soundness via a definitional interpreter, not progress-and-
  preservation; the calculus has no operational semantics here, and terms are
  intrinsically typed, so there is no separate notion of a "stuck" term.

Assumptions.expected is part of the TRUST BASE. A change to it must be reviewed
as a change to what this development assumes -- regenerating it to clear a red
`make check` is exactly how a lemma gets silently admitted, so `make check` also
asserts the golden itself names no assumption but ext_eq.

ASSUMED: functional extensionality (Axioms.ext_eq), and the -impredicative-set
kernel flag, which the whole development is built with.  Impredicative Set is a
non-default extension of Coq's kernel and is known to be inconsistent with
classical excluded middle plus choice, so no classical axiom may ever be added
here.  Nothing else: zero Admitted, zero admit.

NOT PROVED -- do not cite this for any of the following.

  * The compiler.  There is no extraction and no refinement proof.  src/coq is
    not part of the build.  The model here has no formal connection to the
    ~5,200-line elaborator (src/elaborate.sml), which is where disjointness is
    actually decided -- with unification metavariables (CUnif), row guessing and
    CMap normalization that appear nowhere in this model.  disj here is
    DECLARATIVE and proved SOUND only; it is not proved complete, not proved
    decidable, and the compiler's algorithm is not proved to implement it.  A
    bug in that solver is not excluded by anything here.

  * Most of the language.  Absent from the model: the entire SQL layer
    (sql_query, sql_exp, sql_table, sql_subset, sql_from_items and 14 further
    sql_* constructors -- 19 in all); effects and concurrency (transaction, source, signal,
    channel); XML/XHTML; the module system (signatures, functors, CModProj);
    datatypes and pattern matching; type classes; kind polymorphism (KFun,
    CKAbs/CKApp); kind units and tuples (KUnit, KTuple); and general recursion
    (val rec / EDValRec) -- which this model structurally cannot host, because
    eDen is a total structurally-recursive Coq function and the real language
    is Turing-complete.

  * Anything about information flow.  disj_correct is record-field non-aliasing
    inside the model.  It is not unlinkability, not secrecy, not
    noninterference, and it says nothing about UrFlow.
